    {
      "id": "xYfZd5gRZE23rYEZdtQCi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 366,
      "y": 169.5,
      "width": 807.066650390625,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8S",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1499998123,
      "version": 184,
      "versionNonce": 1981383685,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1778547627649,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Exercise 3.14: The following procedure is quite useful, although obscure:\n\n(define (mystery x)\n    (define (loop x y)\n        (if (null? x)\n            y\n            (let ((temp (cdr x)))\n                (set-cdr! x y)\n                (loop temp x))))\n    (loop x '()))\n\nloop uses the “temporary” variable temp to hold the old value of the cdr of x,\nsince the set-cdr! on the next line destroys the cdr. Explain what mystery does\nin general.\nSuppose v is defined by (define v (list 'a 'b 'c 'd)). Draw the box-and-pointer\ndiagram that represents the list to which v is bound. Suppose that we now\nevaluate (define w (mystery v)). Draw box-and-pointer diagrams that show the\nstructures v and w after evaluating this expression. What would be printed as the\nvalues of v and w?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Exercise 3.14: The following procedure is quite useful, although obscure:\n\n(define (mystery x)\n    (define (loop x y)\n        (if (null? x)\n            y\n            (let ((temp (cdr x)))\n                (set-cdr! x y)\n                (loop temp x))))\n    (loop x '()))\n\nloop uses the “temporary” variable temp to hold the old value of the cdr of x,\nsince the set-cdr! on the next line destroys the cdr. Explain what mystery does\nin general.\nSuppose v is defined by (define v (list 'a 'b 'c 'd)). Draw the box-and-pointer\ndiagram that represents the list to which v is bound. Suppose that we now\nevaluate (define w (mystery v)). Draw box-and-pointer diagrams that show the\nstructures v and w after evaluating this expression. What would be printed as the\nvalues of v and w?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
